# Correct only conversion errors where generated output differs from the…

Correct only conversion errors where generated output differs from the
original source evidence.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Correct only conversion errors where generated output differs from the
  original source evidence.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency was recorded in the legacy TODO.

## Evidence

World material publication no longer applies the historical identity-specific
Lard Lad RGB-complement recipe. P3D texture extraction already copies embedded
image payload bytes directly, and no tracked source or conversion evidence
authorized rewriting one shader/file-name pair after extraction. Recovered PNG
bytes are now content-addressed and published unchanged. A typed regression
requires the prepared texture payload to equal the source bytes and derives its
SHA-256/file identity from those same bytes.

Decoded PDDI `DIFF` colour values
now preserve all four authored `0xAARRGGBB` channels as well. The extractor
publishes the source `COLOUR` value unchanged and no tracked format evidence
marks alpha zero as an opaque sentinel, so FBX material conversion no longer
rewrites `0x00112233` to alpha 255. A resolver-level regression requires
`[0x11, 0x22, 0x33, 0x00]`. Strict FBX and pipeline Clippy are clean.

Prop material canonicalization now carries that decoded diffuse RGBA into the
content-derived material binding instead of resetting it to opaque white. A
regression reproduces the former loss with `DIFF=0x11223344` and requires the
canonical binding to retain `[0x22, 0x33, 0x44, 0x11]`. The current extracted
corpus has no used unique shader with a nonwhite `DIFF`, so this closes a latent
lossy boundary without claiming a current cached visual change.

Character material fallback now preserves authored texture identity instead of
rewriting one missing `char_swatches.bmp` reference to the distinct shared
`char_swatches_lit.bmp` source. The current 110-package skinned-character
corpus contains four packages with a plain swatch reference and no local plain
texture. The classified common-rig package publishes `char_swatches_lit.png`
and numbered swatches, but no `char_swatches.png`; its extracted source chunk
is itself named `char_swatches_lit.bmp`. No decoded relationship equates those
two names.

Both the generic FBX external-texture adapter and the package-index
shared-texture lookup therefore require exact normalized file identity, with
regressions rejecting the former cross-name substitution while retaining exact
`char_swatches_lit.bmp` lookup. Missing plain swatch authority now remains
unresolved instead of being repaired to a different authored texture.

The world material path no longer invents an untextured material when a named
analysis shader file is missing. The current extracted corpus contains 12 real
`lambert1` shader declarations and no decoded mesh group requiring a missing
`lambert1` or `Pure3DSimpleShader15` shader. The former exception admitted a
missing file only by name when world texture authority was active, without a
decoded material source. A regression now requires that case to follow ordinary
fail-closed material resolution.

Shared world-texture fallback now retains the decoded shader document instead
of rebuilding a new material binding from its names after local texture lookup
fails. The previous branch discarded authored `DIFF` colour and decoded
transparency, emission, and reflection evidence before canonicalization. The
replacement reuses the exact external-texture resolver, which preserves those
fields while retaining the shader's authored texture identity. A 149-package
terrain-world replay found no currently used unique shader taking this fallback,
so the change hardens a latent boundary without claiming a current output delta.

Character body-atlas selection now also requires every selected body shader to
have its own resolved source texture. Of the four current packages with an
unresolved plain `char_swatches.bmp` reference, `h-fat-m` and `l-cool-m` also
carry exact shared lit-swatch body shaders; the former selector could skip the
unresolved plain binding and continue from those lit pixels. `male5-m` and
`mobstr-m` already had no alternate body source and therefore failed empty. The
new invariant keeps all four plain-swatch gaps explicit instead of allowing a
different body shader to supply atlas pixels.

This umbrella task remains open for other reported conversion differences that
need direct source-backed evidence before any correction is admitted.
